{
  "//": "§2.3 a leader's grant to a member, {\"kind\":\"party\",\"grant\":{...}} in proof.admit. pg = LP(\"AFKLINK-PARTY-GRANT-V1\") || LP(device_id) || LP(key_id 32) || LP(telegram_user_id decimal) || LP(name or \"\") || LP(username or \"\") || LP(\"guest\") || LP(party_id 16, never empty) || LP(grant_id 16) || LP(grant_token 16, or \"\") || LP(issued_at u64 BE ms) || LP(by 32); sig = ECDSA-P256-SHA256 by the leader's device key: the keys' grant V2 (AFKLINK-GRANT-V2) field for field, but for the domain, field 7 and by (main: owner_grant_v2_hex, differs_from_v2_at). The PC's checks in §5.1's order, the first failure as ended_detail of admit_refused: grant_malformed (form: v 1, every field present, name/username null or <= 128/64 Unicode code points — the unit the released grant schema counts in — and well-formed (a case with admit_text carries the JSON text itself), role a string, party_id 16 bytes, grant_token \"\" or 16 bytes, at most 2048 bytes of compact JSON), grant_device, grant_key_mismatch (key_id != proof_key_id), party_leader (no delegation of party_id in pc.delegations, or by != its leader.key_id), grant_role (role != \"guest\"), grant_spent (grant_id in pc.spent_grant_ids), grant_role (owner_on_pc: telegram_user_id in pc.owners_on_pc), grant_bad_sig (under the delegation's leader.spki), grant_stale (grant_token != pc.grant_token); else ok — a base for the list rules (check_add), which scenarios cover. A case's pc overrides the file's. pg_hex: the bytes rebuilt from the case's JSON, and sig_valid: whether sig is by's signature over them (both absent where the JSON was altered after signing). role_owner: the PC's grant_role at step 5, as the keys' grant.json role_owner; a strict client schema refuses the shape. cross_domain: the same fields (inv_id = party_id) signed under one domain — valid there (own_hex), not under the other (other_hex). Consumers assert both the result (expect) and `why` of every negative case: several cases are built so that a verifier missing one rule reaches a different result, and `why` names the rule that must refuse first.",
